/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   PATCH · team_members — let an administrator write the access flag

   AGAINST THE RULES YOU ARE ACTUALLY RUNNING, not a fresh file. Replace the
   existing `match /team_members/{memberId}` block with the one below. Nothing
   else in your rules changes.

   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   WHY THIS IS NEEDED — the Suspend button is refused today
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Your deployed block reads:

       allow create: ... && request.resource.data.email == request.auth.token.email
                         && memberId == request.resource.data.orgId + '__' + request.auth.token.email;
       allow update: ... && resource.data.email == request.auth.token.email
       allow delete: if false;

   Both write clauses require the target document's email to be the CALLER'S
   OWN. That is correct and deliberate for a profile — it is what stops one
   tenant's staff editing another's. But it means nobody, including a ClearSky
   administrator, can write anyone else's team_members doc.

   So the ops console's Suspend button currently fails with permission-denied
   against every user. Not "writes an inert flag" — it does not write at all.
   The console reports the refusal honestly rather than showing a false
   success, but the button cannot do its job until this is deployed.

   Reading is fine as-is: isAdmin() already covers it.

   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   WHAT CHANGES, EXACTLY
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   1. `allow create, update: if isOmegaAdmin()` is added as an alternative.
      isOmegaAdmin(), not isAdmin(), to match every other administrative
      clause in your file — one definition of "may act", which is the whole
      point of omega_staff.

   2. The member's OWN self-update is tightened by exactly one clause: they
      may no longer write the access fields. Without that, a suspended user's
      next ordinary profile save sets active back to true and un-suspends
      them — the enforcement would be real and undone by accident.

   3. `allow delete: if false` is unchanged. Deleting a member doc would
      erase the suspension along with the person.

   Everything else — the orgId pin, the demo bucket, the email pin, the
   memberId format check — is carried over verbatim.
   ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

    match /team_members/{memberId} {

      /* The fields the ops console owns. `active` is in here because it is
         what any enforcement reads, and a member who can write their own
         `active` can undo their own suspension with one profile save. */
      function accessFields() {
        return ['active', 'access', 'suspendedAt', 'suspendedBy', 'suspendedReason',
                'passwordResetSentAt', 'passwordResetSentBy', 'updatedBy'];
      }
      function touchesAccess() {
        return request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasAny(accessFields());
      }

      allow read:   if signedIn() && (resource.data.orgId == userOrg()
                                      || ownsDemoBucket(resource.data.orgId)
                                      || isAdmin());

      /* UNCHANGED except for the isOmegaAdmin() alternative: a member still
         self-creates their own profile, pinned to their own email and the
         "<orgId>__<email>" id format. The console needs create too, because
         suspending somebody who has never opened the portal writes the first
         version of their doc. */
      allow create: if isOmegaAdmin()
                    || (signedIn()
                        && (request.resource.data.orgId == userOrg()
                            || ownsDemoBucket(request.resource.data.orgId))
                        && request.resource.data.email == request.auth.token.email
                        && memberId == request.resource.data.orgId + '__' + request.auth.token.email
                        /* Nobody signs themselves up pre-suspended or
                           pre-cleared; the flag is the console's to set. */
                        && !('access' in request.resource.data));

      allow update: if isOmegaAdmin()
                    || (signedIn()
                        && (resource.data.orgId == userOrg()
                            || ownsDemoBucket(resource.data.orgId))
                        && resource.data.email == request.auth.token.email
                        && request.resource.data.orgId == resource.data.orgId
                        && request.resource.data.email == resource.data.email
                        && !touchesAccess());          /* ← the one addition */

      allow delete: if false;
    }


/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   OPTIONAL · omega_access_log — read the audit trail

   Only needed if you deploy /functions. The Admin SDK bypasses rules, so the
   functions can write this collection with no rule at all; this block exists
   so an administrator can READ it. Without it the log accumulates and only the
   Firebase console can see it.

   Writes are refused for everyone on purpose: a browser session that could
   forge or erase an entry would make the trail worthless.
   ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

    match /omega_access_log/{entryId} {
      allow read:  if isOmegaAdmin();
      allow write: if false;
    }


/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   NOT A PATCH · two things worth a decision, neither changed here
   ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

   1 · isConsoleViewer() grants sunesol.com and ogisolar.com a cross-org read
       of EVERY tenant's /projects. Your own file already flags this and asks
       for a decision before OGI Solar's trial opens.

       It matters more now than it did when that note was written. The
       Accounts page reads /projects across orgs and screens what it finds —
       so the same data those two domains can already read is now visibly
       collated into "what every tenant is building, and how big". The leak
       was always there; this makes its value obvious.

       If OGI Solar is a restricted trial tenant, remove them from
       isConsoleViewer() before the trial opens. isOmegaStaff() on the same
       clause already covers ClearSky's own people, so removing both non-staff
       domains costs the console nothing.

   2 · omega_orgs and equipment are readable on `orgId == userOrg() || isAdmin()`
       — NOT isOmegaStaff(). So a rep on a non-ClearSky domain signing into the
       console gets:
         · no tenant display names (falls back to title-cased domains)
         · no equipment registry, so placed items in editor projects size at
           zero and read as "Needs sizing"

       Everything else works for them. If reps on other domains are meant to
       use the Accounts page, add `|| isOmegaStaff()` to the READ clause of
       both — read only, not write. Left alone here because widening a read
       across every tenant is your call, not a patch to apply unread.
   ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */
